 ;; MTH 251 — Differential Calculus
 {:course/id #uuid "b2e5af5c-39d0-53ab-8fc6-a2a70c44d25e"
  :course/title "Differential Calculus"
  :course/code "MTH 251"
  :course/school "Oregon State University"
  :course/description "Differential Calculus introduces the mathematics of change for real-valued functions of one variable. Building on algebra and trigonometry, the course develops the concepts and methods needed to interpret derivatives, analyze functions, and solve problems involving changing quantities. Graphical, numerical, and symbolic representations connect computational techniques with mathematical reasoning and applications."
  :course/overview "Limits and continuity provide the conceptual framework for studying derivatives. Students connect average rates of change with instantaneous rates, then use limit laws and differentiation rules—including the chain rule—to calculate and interpret derivatives. The emphasis is on understanding what these calculations represent, rather than treating differentiation solely as algebraic manipulation.\n\nApplications connect derivatives to motion, the shape of a graph, and maximum and minimum problems. Students use calculus to analyze velocity, speed, and acceleration; investigate how functions change; and solve optimization problems. Mathematical modeling includes identifying relevant variables, making appropriate assumptions, and evaluating whether a method and its conclusions make sense in context."
  :course/outcomes [
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "e11ac0b7-168a-53f3-9ca4-4dc90089617e"
     :course-outcome/index 1
     :course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives"
     :course-outcome/text "Evaluate limits using algebraic limit laws and interpret limiting behavior through graphical, numerical, and symbolic representations."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "f6479bab-c0ae-5920-bba4-64293ec407a7"
     :course-outcome/index 2
     :course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives"
     :course-outcome/text "Analyze the continuity of a single-variable function at a point or over an interval."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "e859a4bd-288c-5aef-9572-3dcf309d9852"
     :course-outcome/index 3
     :course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives"
     :course-outcome/text "Calculate average and instantaneous rates of change and explain how the derivative represents an instantaneous rate."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "8e0b28cf-8e52-5504-95a8-4e9b239c85a5"
     :course-outcome/index 4
     :course-outcome/category "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives"
     :course-outcome/text "Differentiate single-variable functions using standard differentiation rules, including the chain rule."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "6b02704b-77f0-59f1-b300-7d1ad5522589"
     :course-outcome/index 5
     :course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications"
     :course-outcome/text "Use derivatives to analyze motion, including relationships among position, velocity, speed, and acceleration."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "794f739d-0e50-5e45-952a-ca590ac4b91e"
     :course-outcome/index 6
     :course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications"
     :course-outcome/text "Determine the shape and behavior of a graph using derivative information, including increasing and decreasing behavior, extrema, and concavity."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "219db5a6-b013-55dc-84f6-a6af421dbaa2"
     :course-outcome/index 7
     :course-outcome/category "Function Analysis and Applications"
     :course-outcome/text "Formulate and solve maximum and minimum problems using differential calculus."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "5d9d7b2f-fee2-5bef-9ba6-2ce5add42a5d"
     :course-outcome/index 8
     :course-outcome/category "Mathematical Modeling and Reasoning"
     :course-outcome/text "Recognize situations that can be modeled mathematically, identify relevant quantities, and calculate or estimate the relationships needed to solve a problem."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "0492ff25-34f8-58e0-8deb-997fcf89ad94"
     :course-outcome/index 9
     :course-outcome/category "Mathematical Modeling and Reasoning"
     :course-outcome/text "Evaluate the suitability of a mathematical approach and the validity of its conclusions, accounting for the assumptions used in constructing the model."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
   ]
  :db/ensure :course/identity-validate}

 ;; MTH 252 — Integral Calculus
 {:course/id #uuid "72faeb91-1b34-5252-b9a7-456659fc2b8c"
  :course/title "Integral Calculus"
  :course/code "MTH 252"
  :course/school "Oregon State University"
  :course/description "Integral Calculus studies accumulation and integration for functions of one variable. Building on differential calculus, the course develops definite and indefinite integrals and their applications. Graphical, numerical, and symbolic approaches support the interpretation of integrals and their use in mathematical modeling and problem solving."
  :course/overview "The course connects Riemann sums, definite integrals, and antiderivatives through the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Students develop integration techniques and use numerical approximation alongside analytical methods. These tools support applications to areas, volumes, curve lengths, motion, force, and work. Logarithmic and exponential functions, polar coordinates, improper integrals, and elementary growth and decay models extend the range of problems addressed."
  :course/outcomes [
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "e1fe3e3d-fdc9-57c6-82fe-78d2079e4886"
     :course-outcome/index 1
     :course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration"
     :course-outcome/text "Interpret Riemann sums and definite integrals graphically and as signed accumulation."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "9d9f2f48-039f-5217-b7b2-a6b64a7b94c2"
     :course-outcome/index 2
     :course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration"
     :course-outcome/text "Find antiderivatives and distinguish indefinite integrals from definite integrals."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "0f810e7c-6d63-5dc7-a416-6c8e6b1e0913"
     :course-outcome/index 3
     :course-outcome/category "Foundations of Integration"
     :course-outcome/text "Apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate definite integrals and differentiate integrals with variable limits."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "9e16c6c4-7123-5514-bfa3-5e97b7f45018"
     :course-outcome/index 4
     :course-outcome/category "Integration Methods"
     :course-outcome/text "Select and apply substitution, integration by parts, trigonometric integration methods, and partial fractions."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "a998873a-721a-57ac-b84c-6a7a8dcef7be"
