    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "3b8afaeb-63e0-509b-8cdd-cc346c364684"
     :course-outcome/index 8
     :course-outcome/category "Geometric and Physical Applications"
     :course-outcome/text "Use integration to calculate curve lengths and physical quantities, including displacement, total distance, force, and work."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "3baf45c7-8ce5-5b29-804e-716fa9a6b746"
     :course-outcome/index 9
     :course-outcome/category "Geometric and Physical Applications"
     :course-outcome/text "Formulate and solve elementary first-order differential equations describing growth and decay."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
   ]
  :db/ensure :course/identity-validate}

 ;; MTH 254 — Vector Calculus I
 {:course/id #uuid "e9dc19ff-a53b-51f7-bd17-d1b14351322a"
  :course/title "Vector Calculus I"
  :course/code "MTH 254"
  :course/school "Oregon State University"
  :course/description "Vector Calculus I extends differentiation and integration to vector-valued functions and functions of several variables. Building on single-variable calculus, the course develops mathematical tools for describing curves, surfaces, motion, and quantities distributed across regions in two or three dimensions. Geometric interpretation and physical applications connect vector operations, multivariable derivatives, and multiple integrals."
  :course/overview "Vectors and coordinate systems provide a language for describing geometry in the plane and in space. Vector-valued functions represent curves and motion, while derivatives and integrals of these functions describe velocity, acceleration, arc length, and curvature. Equations of surfaces and their level sets extend graphical reasoning to functions of several variables.\n\nMultivariable differentiation includes limits, continuity, partial derivatives, the chain rule, directional derivatives, and gradients. These concepts support tangent-plane approximations and optimization, including constrained problems using Lagrange multipliers. Multiple integration addresses accumulated quantities over planar and spatial regions. Rectangular, polar, cylindrical, and spherical coordinates—and more general changes of variables—allow students to match an integral’s representation to the geometry of its region."
  :course/outcomes [
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "1f022312-9abe-5b04-a5f8-7ef76765a398"
     :course-outcome/index 1
     :course-outcome/category "Vectors, Coordinates, and Spatial Geometry"
     :course-outcome/text "Describe points and regions using rectangular, polar, cylindrical, and spherical coordinates and convert between appropriate coordinate systems."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "65d6478b-61f1-5af6-9344-7d002c435d42"
     :course-outcome/index 2
     :course-outcome/category "Vectors, Coordinates, and Spatial Geometry"
     :course-outcome/text "Perform vector operations, including dot and cross products, and interpret their geometric meaning."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "d8b2769d-cef4-5b6e-aaec-ef6eff7468e9"
     :course-outcome/index 3
     :course-outcome/category "Vectors, Coordinates, and Spatial Geometry"
     :course-outcome/text "Represent and analyze lines, planes, surfaces, and level sets using equations and geometric descriptions."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "3380e0d8-0f1a-5f51-9026-c5faef1771a8"
     :course-outcome/index 4
     :course-outcome/category "Vector-Valued Functions and Curves"
     :course-outcome/text "Differentiate and integrate vector-valued functions and use them to describe position, velocity, and acceleration."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "d027fd3a-14c1-57ad-b934-a1539ea5111c"
     :course-outcome/index 5
     :course-outcome/category "Vector-Valued Functions and Curves"
     :course-outcome/text "Calculate arc length and curvature and interpret tangent and normal vectors as descriptions of a curve’s local geometry."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "47d38675-dd90-5ae2-902d-f0824b76ad82"
     :course-outcome/index 6
     :course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization"
     :course-outcome/text "Evaluate limits and analyze continuity for functions of several variables."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "d0c55d89-b69c-5141-ab2a-14bcfa56b84a"
     :course-outcome/index 7
     :course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization"
     :course-outcome/text "Calculate partial derivatives and apply the multivariable chain rule."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "2c4ed1eb-3abb-55f1-b187-73c15346e09b"
     :course-outcome/index 8
     :course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization"
     :course-outcome/text "Use gradients and directional derivatives to determine rates and directions of change and relate them to level sets."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "9a78a88e-3c1e-5179-a3a0-5bb4ef9e8a5b"
     :course-outcome/index 9
     :course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization"
     :course-outcome/text "Construct tangent planes and use linear approximations to estimate nearby function values."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "9c17afa0-6af5-5717-9945-124a8e2c5fe9"
     :course-outcome/index 10
     :course-outcome/category "Multivariable Differentiation and Optimization"
     :course-outcome/text "Find and classify critical points and solve unconstrained and constrained optimization problems, including problems requiring Lagrange multipliers."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "48a43247-d19d-5bcb-b5ca-c75ddbf4080d"
     :course-outcome/index 11
     :course-outcome/category "Multiple Integration"
     :course-outcome/text "Set up and evaluate double and triple integrals over specified regions."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "50dd9302-e5bf-5bd3-90be-5e44b6db61fa"
     :course-outcome/index 12
     :course-outcome/category "Multiple Integration"
     :course-outcome/text "Select suitable coordinates and apply changes of variables, including the appropriate area or volume scaling factor."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "7c28ceff-35c9-5d29-a46e-92392c3024c6"
     :course-outcome/index 13
     :course-outcome/category "Multiple Integration"
     :course-outcome/text "Use multiple integrals to calculate geometric and physical quantities, including volume and mass."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
   ]
  :db/ensure :course/identity-validate}

 ;; MTH 255 — Vector Calculus II
 {:course/id #uuid "c37c596e-77c4-5554-8295-443fb5da076b"
  :course/title "Vector Calculus II"
  :course/code "MTH 255"
  :course/school "Oregon State University"
  :course/description "Vector Calculus II develops the calculus of vector fields and integration along curves and over surfaces. Building on Vector Calculus I, the course connects local descriptions of a field with accumulated quantities such as work, circulation, and flux. Its central tools include conservative fields, divergence, curl, and the integral theorems of Gauss and Stokes, with applications involving force, work, mass, and charge."
  :course/overview "The course revisits vector functions, space curves, gradients, and directional derivatives before extending integration to curves and surfaces. Line integrals connect vector fields with work and circulation. Conservative fields, potential functions, and path independence explain when an integral can be determined from endpoint information rather than the details of a path.\n\nSurface geometry and oriented surface elements provide the foundation for surface integrals and flux. Divergence and curl describe local field behavior, while the Divergence Theorem and Stokes’ Theorem connect these local quantities to integrals over regions and their boundaries. The archived course materials emphasize geometric understanding and the use of vector differentials to connect the various integration methods."
  :course/outcomes [
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "3561ac1e-b8dd-59c8-b44a-28a8ad8f9fdc"
     :course-outcome/index 1
     :course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation"
     :course-outcome/text "Express vectors in standard coordinate systems and bases and interpret dot and cross products geometrically."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "b93f8b7e-2e08-5fbc-b3b3-5fd74c01da7c"
     :course-outcome/index 2
     :course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation"
     :course-outcome/text "Explain the relationship among gradients, directional derivatives, and level sets."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "6ad6c8c5-38f2-5456-9c16-b54686cf0083"
     :course-outcome/index 3
     :course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation"
     :course-outcome/text "Construct line and surface elements appropriate to a curve or surface, accounting for its geometry and orientation."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "da10e544-4158-5cc3-9189-961bf8e19bf4"
     :course-outcome/index 4
     :course-outcome/category "Line Integrals and Conservative Fields"
     :course-outcome/text "Evaluate scalar and vector line integrals and interpret their use in calculating quantities such as mass, work, and circulation."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "9cd133e0-c54b-57f1-b597-4c2164a5bb07"
     :course-outcome/index 5
     :course-outcome/category "Line Integrals and Conservative Fields"
     :course-outcome/text "Analyze conservative fields, find potential functions when they exist, and use path independence to evaluate line integrals."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "c94983bd-7208-5af7-ab33-1ac7d94075a5"
     :course-outcome/index 6
     :course-outcome/category "Surface Integrals and Flux"
     :course-outcome/text "Set up and evaluate scalar surface integrals for quantities distributed over a surface, including mass or charge."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "25423c4b-02b3-5fec-9546-7129ca1e1f95"
     :course-outcome/index 7
     :course-outcome/category "Surface Integrals and Flux"
     :course-outcome/text "Calculate flux through an oriented surface and interpret the role of the field’s normal component."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "68ca80d7-e11e-58c2-9696-309503e57498"
     :course-outcome/index 8
     :course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems"
     :course-outcome/text "Calculate divergence and curl in standard coordinate systems and interpret them in relation to flux and circulation."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "8b26130c-3ade-59f1-b5cd-30c4e77a4ee2"
     :course-outcome/index 9
     :course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems"
     :course-outcome/text "Explain and apply the Divergence Theorem to connect flux through a closed surface with a volume integral."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "11744f40-4941-5b05-bf74-21cff6dfbc86"
     :course-outcome/index 10
     :course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems"
     :course-outcome/text "Explain and apply Stokes’ Theorem to connect circulation around a boundary curve with a surface integral of curl."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
   ]
  :db/ensure :course/identity-validate}

 ;; MTH 256 — Applied Differential Equations
 {:course/id #uuid "fa274c9b-ca65-5fde-b8ed-49681698e235"
  :course/title "Applied Differential Equations"
  :course/code "MTH 256"
  :course/school "Oregon State University"
  :course/description "Applied Differential Equations introduces methods for describing and solving problems in which an unknown function is related to its derivatives. The course focuses on ordinary differential equations and applications appropriate to science and engineering, particularly electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators. Coverage includes first-order linear and selected nonlinear equations, second-order linear equations, and introductory work with higher-order equations and Laplace transforms."
  :course/overview "The archived syllabus begins with mathematical modeling and the classification of differential equations, then develops methods for first-order and second-order equations. This organization connects the structure of an equation with an appropriate solution approach. Applications give meaning to the unknown functions and their derivatives rather than treating equations as purely formal expressions.\n\nLaplace transforms provide an additional approach to solving differential equations, while introductory higher-order work extends the range of equations considered. Electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators serve as central application settings. The published description also identifies familiarity with complex numbers and Euler’s identities as relevant background."
  :course/outcomes [
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "b3f87601-f5f0-5265-a60a-d5faa7f8ef26"
     :course-outcome/index 1
     :course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling"
     :course-outcome/text "Classify ordinary differential equations by order and distinguish linear equations from nonlinear equations."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "06f1cd43-7150-5138-9257-b06e58024ccf"
     :course-outcome/index 2
     :course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling"
     :course-outcome/text "Translate a basic scientific or engineering model into a differential equation relating an unknown function to its rates of change."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "a8623707-6d3b-55ce-b413-a7dcb5686dd4"
     :course-outcome/index 3
     :course-outcome/category "Equation Classification and Mathematical Modeling"
     :course-outcome/text "Interpret the variables, derivatives, and solutions of a differential-equation model in the context of the problem."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "5879a21c-ee5c-5351-9547-766af3a0a0b4"
     :course-outcome/index 4
     :course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods"
     :course-outcome/text "Solve first-order linear equations and selected first-order nonlinear equations using methods appropriate to their form."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "f3d63c4b-0f30-5288-8faf-fd7effcd7d55"
     :course-outcome/index 5
     :course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods"
     :course-outcome/text "Obtain solutions of second-order linear differential equations."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "b27b909d-406b-5016-9dba-6d6cde8fe738"
     :course-outcome/index 6
     :course-outcome/category "Analytical Solution Methods"
     :course-outcome/text "Apply introductory solution methods to higher-order linear differential equations."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "d14ff5cc-c727-5ac4-80dc-396644cb4d2d"
     :course-outcome/index 7
     :course-outcome/category "Laplace Transform Methods"
     :course-outcome/text "Explain how a Laplace transform can convert a linear differential-equation problem into an algebraic problem."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "1a7956a5-b684-551c-83df-ccea47ac1ba4"
     :course-outcome/index 8
     :course-outcome/category "Laplace Transform Methods"
     :course-outcome/text "Use elementary Laplace transforms and inverse transforms to solve basic linear differential-equation problems."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "7677d590-eded-5f0e-a320-fd5a22fef284"
     :course-outcome/index 9
     :course-outcome/category "Electrical and Mechanical Applications"
     :course-outcome/text "Formulate and solve differential-equation problems involving electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators, interpreting the resulting functions in terms of the modeled system."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
   ]
  :db/ensure :course/identity-validate}

 ;; MTH 341 — Linear Algebra I
 {:course/id #uuid "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7"
  :course/title "Linear Algebra I"
  :course/code "MTH 341"
  :course/school "Oregon State University"
  :course/description "Linear Algebra I introduces the computational and conceptual study of linear systems, matrices, and subspaces. The course connects methods for solving equations with the structure of linear transformations and vector spaces. Determinants, linear independence, bases, dimension, and introductory eigenvalue analysis provide complementary ways to understand how a linear system or transformation behaves."
  :course/overview "Work with linear systems and Gaussian elimination establishes a foundation for matrix operations, invertibility, and determinants. The course then connects these calculations with linear independence, spanning sets, subspaces, bases, and dimension. Row, column, and null spaces describe important features of a matrix, while rank–nullity relates those spaces to the structure of a system’s solutions.\n\nMatrix representations make linear transformations concrete through examples such as rotation, reflection, projection, and scaling. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors introduce directions preserved by a transformation, including examples with complex eigenvalues. The archived section used here also introduces diagonalization and illustrates eigenvalue methods through simple systems of differential equations."
  :course/outcomes [
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "15723e90-f0cc-5ee0-9f2e-05c5f5aed323"
     :course-outcome/index 1
     :course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation"
     :course-outcome/text "Use Gaussian elimination to solve linear systems and describe their complete solution sets."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "3ef6ac4e-6cad-594f-83a6-5f66d9769392"
     :course-outcome/index 2
     :course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation"
     :course-outcome/text "Perform matrix operations and determine whether a square matrix has an inverse, calculating it when one exists."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "26f3092c-2231-5b66-80de-d4a832531472"
     :course-outcome/index 3
     :course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation"
     :course-outcome/text "Calculate determinants and use their properties to assess invertibility and the uniqueness of solutions to square linear systems."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "753ae7d6-d7b2-545e-b987-f70e367561c7"
     :course-outcome/index 4
     :course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension"
     :course-outcome/text "Determine whether a subset of \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) satisfies the requirements for a subspace."
     :db/ensure :course-outcome/validate}
    {:course-outcome/id #uuid "d28777c9-3775-500a-8e78-8f1ec68fe02a"
     :course-outcome/index 5
     :course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension"
